  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/_en-19/",
      "html": "<p>The Doradidae family contains around 90 species in about 35 genera. Thus they represent only a minority among the huge number of catfishes existing in South America. But all doradid catfishes are really bizarre and desirable creatures for catfish enthusiasts.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/_en-19/",
      "html": "<p>Origin: Paraguay.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/_en-19/",
      "html": "<p>Regarding food and water parameters O. stuebelii are totally unpretentious and represent a hardy beginner's species. One should only keep in mind that they originate from subtropical areas and should not be kept too warm year in, year out.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/_en-19/",
      "html": "<p>Many doradid catfish are nocturnal and hide during the day. This is not true for O. stuebelii, which is pretty active during the day. Towards congeners O. stuebelii are totally peaceful, and although they cannot be termed schooling fish they like to stay together in groups.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10c-catfishes-remaining-catfish-from-south-america/_en-19/",
      "html": "<p>There are doradid catfishes that reach around one metre in length, but for Opsodoras stuebelii only 10-15 cm total length are reported. The specimens described here originate from Paraguay and were determined using the standard aquarium literature; however, there is some indication that these fish in truth represent a species completely new to science (the \"real\" Opsodoras stuebelii has a much longer snout). Among insiders of the \"catfish scene\" this fish is therefore also known as \"Doradidae sp. 2\".</p>"
    }
  ],
